  <p><strong>How do brands prevent garment dye from bleeding into a white heat-transfer logo?</strong></p>

  <h2>Recognise the failure before replacing the logo</h2>
  <p>A pale logo can pick up colour from the underlying dyed or sublimated polyester. Compare the white area on the garment with an unpressed reference under the same light. A logo that was clear at the press can look different later, so agree a hold time and photograph it both immediately and at the end of that window.</p>

  <h2>Approve the full application combination</h2>
  <p>Request trials on the intended face fabric, colour and finish. Have the decorator propose a compatible dye-barrier transfer and confirm application settings for that transfer and substrate. A lower-temperature process may be an option for some systems. Altering the temperature without checking adhesion can trade discolouration for lifting edges.</p>

  <h2>Separate migration from other failure modes</h2>
  <p>After the agreed care cycle, evaluate the transfer for tint, edge lift and cracking under representative stretch. A tinted but intact print and a clean print with peeling edges require different follow-up. Record the tested fabric lot and transfer reference so a new colour, fabric finish or transfer batch can be reviewed before release.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.siser.com/digital-printing/sublithinmatt-lt/">Siser’s technical information on a dye-barrier transfer</a>.</p>
